    question_text: 'Alexandra Petrov, CFA, is a senior portfolio manager at Horizon Capital Management, overseeing $4.2 billion in institutional assets. During a quarterly review meeting, her largest client, the $850 million Riverside Pension Fund, requests that Petrov exclude all tobacco, firearms, and fossil fuel companies from their portfolio. Petrov personally disagrees with these exclusions, believing they will significantly reduce diversification and expected returns by approximately 120 basis points annually based on her quantitative models. The pension fund\'s investment policy statement, written five years ago, contains no reference to environmental, social, or governance (ESG) criteria or ethical screening. Petrov\'s firm has no formal policy on handling ESG requests. After the meeting, Petrov implements the exclusions but documents in the client file that she believes this approach violates her fiduciary duty to maximize risk-adjusted returns. She also sends the client a formal letter outlining the expected performance impact. Six months later, the portfolio underperforms its benchmark by 95 basis points, which Petrov attributes partly to the exclusions. Which of the following statements regarding Petrov\'s actions is least accurate?',
