    question_text: 'Rafael Dominguez, CFA, is a fixed income portfolio manager at Sovereign Wealth Advisors, managing a $1.8 billion emerging markets debt fund. Dominguez receives a research report from his firm\'s credit analyst suggesting that Venezuela government bonds, which constitute 4.2% of his fund\'s holdings (approximately $75 million), face imminent default risk due to deteriorating political conditions and depleted foreign currency reserves. The analyst recommends immediate liquidation. However, Dominguez knows that his fund is the largest holder of these specific bonds, and any attempt to sell would likely trigger a market panic, causing prices to collapse by an estimated 30-40% before he could exit the full position. Instead of selling immediately, Dominguez implements the following strategy: (1) He gradually reduces the position over three weeks by selling small blocks of $2-3 million daily to avoid moving the market; (2) He does not disclose the analyst\'s default warning in his weekly investor letter, reasoning that premature disclosure could trigger redemptions and force fire sales; (3) He contacts three other emerging markets fund managers whom he knows also hold Venezuelan debt, informally suggesting they "consider taking profits" without explicitly sharing the default analysis; (4) During this three-week period, several new investors purchase shares in his fund at NAV, effectively buying into a portfolio with significant Venezuelan exposure; (5) By the time Dominguez fully exits the position, he has limited the fund\'s loss to 18% on the Venezuelan holdings instead of the 35% loss that would have resulted from immediate liquidation. Two months later, Venezuela announces a debt restructuring, validating the analyst\'s concerns. Which of the following Standards has Dominguez most likely violated?',
    option_a: 'Standard II(A) Material Nonpublic Information, because he acted on the analyst\'s confidential research about Venezuela\'s impending default before the information was publicly available, and he selectively communicated this information to other fund managers who could trade on it',
