    explanation: 'Option A is most consistent with Standard I(C) Misrepresentation and Standard I(D) Misconduct. When asked to provide a professional reference, CFA Institute members have an obligation to be honest and not misrepresent material facts, even when doing so may harm personal or business relationships. Option C would violate Standard I(C) by providing a misleadingly positive reference that omits material concerns about Torres\'s conduct and performance. While the search committee bears responsibility for due diligence, Wu also has an ethical obligation not to affirmatively misrepresent Torres\'s qualifications when specifically asked for her assessment. Providing a knowingly incomplete picture could mislead the hiring organization and harm the beneficiaries of that pension fund. Option B appears cautious but is less desirable than Option A. While declining to serve as a reference is permissible, it may unfairly harm Torres when Wu actually has relevant knowledge of his professional capabilities. The conflict of interest (Torres being a major client) can be managed through disclosure rather than avoidance. Option A properly balances multiple considerations: (1) it fulfills Wu\'s duty of honesty by providing material information about both strengths and concerns; (2) it discloses the conflict of interest created by the client relationship, allowing the search committee to weight her feedback appropriately; (3) it respects Torres by acknowledging his genuine strengths while noting areas of concern; (4) it protects the interests of the hiring organization\'s beneficiaries by providing complete information. The key principle is that CFA Institute members cannot compartmentalize their ethical obligations - the duty to be honest and not misrepresent facts applies even when the member is acting in a sales or relationship management capacity rather than as an investment professional. Wu cannot provide a misleading reference simply because it would benefit her business interests or personal relationship. The Standards require integrity in all professional activities, including serving as a professional reference. Full disclosure of both material positives and concerns, along with acknowledgment of potential bias, represents the most ethical approach.',
