    question_text: 'Dmitri Volkov, CFA, is a founding partner of Apex Emerging Markets Fund, a hedge fund specializing in frontier market equities with $680 million in assets under management. The fund charges a 2% management fee and 20% performance fee above an 8% hurdle rate. Volkov and his two co-founders collectively own 100% of the management company and have personal investments of $15 million each in the fund alongside client capital. During due diligence on a potential $85 million investment in Kazakh Energy Corporation, Volkov\'s research team identifies significant concerns about corporate governance, including: (1) the CEO is the brother-in-law of Kazakhstan\'s Energy Minister; (2) two of seven board members have undisclosed business relationships with the CEO; (3) the company\'s auditor, a local firm, has limited international experience; (4) related-party transactions totaling $140 million annually are inadequately disclosed in financial statements; (5) the company refuses to provide quarterly earnings calls or detailed segment reporting. However, Volkov\'s valuation analysis suggests the company is trading at 4.2x earnings versus comparable companies at 12-15x, representing significant upside potential of 180-200% if governance concerns are addressed or if the market simply re-rates the company. Volkov notes that several respected emerging market investors have recently initiated positions. Volkov proposes a strategy: invest $85 million (12.5% of fund assets) in Kazakh Energy, making Apex one of the largest institutional shareholders, then use this position to engage with management and push for governance reforms including independent directors, international audit firm, and improved disclosure. He plans to disclose in the fund\'s quarterly letter that Kazakh Energy is a top position and that governance engagement is ongoing. Volkov\'s partners are divided - one supports the investment as consistent with the fund\'s value-oriented, activist approach, while the other opposes it as exposing clients to excessive governance risk for the benefit of generating performance fees. The fund\'s offering memorandum states that it invests in "undervalued emerging market equities" and may take "significant positions to influence corporate governance," but provides no specific guidelines on governance standards or position size limits. Which of the following statements regarding Volkov\'s proposed investment is most accurate?',
