    explanation: 'Option A is most accurate. Standard III(A) requires members to act with loyalty, prudence, and care when managing client assets. While value investing and activism are legitimate strategies, the combination of severe governance red flags (political connections, related-party transactions, inadequate disclosure, related board members, questionable audit quality) and large position size (12.5% of fund assets) creates imprudent risk concentration. The key question is whether a reasonable fiduciary would concentrate $85 million of client assets in a single company with such pervasive governance deficiencies, regardless of valuation discount. The governance issues at Kazakh Energy are not isolated problems but systemic deficiencies suggesting potential fraud risk, expropriation of minority shareholders, or financial statement unreliability. A 180-200% upside scenario assumes governance can be reformed or the market will look past these issues, but both assumptions are speculative. The fact that Volkov stands to earn 20% of gains above the hurdle rate creates a conflict - he has asymmetric incentives to take governance risk (unlimited upside participation, limited downside given the hurdle rate). Option B is insufficient because disclosure cannot cure a fundamental imprudence. While transparency is required, Standard III(A)\'s duty of care means some investments may be inappropriate regardless of disclosure. Detailed quarterly letter disclosure after the investment is made does not address whether the initial investment decision was prudent. Option C misapplies Standard III(C). The fact that the offering memorandum permits activism and significant positions does not mean any investment satisfying these criteria is suitable. Suitability requires consideration of the specific risks and whether they are appropriate for the fund\'s clients. Volkov\'s personal capital at risk does not justify exposing client capital to the same imprudent concentration - if anything, his personal wealth and access to other investments mean he can afford to take risks that may be inappropriate for clients. The 12.5% position size in a single frontier market company with pervasive governance deficiencies likely exceeds the bounds of prudent portfolio management, regardless of valuation or disclosure. A more appropriate approach would be a smaller initial position (2-3% of assets) with provision to increase after demonstrating successful governance engagement.',
